>Dan Zerkle  zerkle@iris.eecs.ucdavis.edu  writes:
>proposal to address the polypiling issue by the elimination of
>the generation of w.o.p. in polypiles.

>Any objections?

  Yes. First of all polypiling is a lot of fun. This proposed change
would make the game more difficult but I don't see it as making the
game more fun to play. The idea of eliminating the polymorph of wands
into wands of polymorph (and wands of wishing for that matter) seems
to me to introduce a logical inconsistancy into the game. In addition
you are thereby eliminating a good strategy to acquire inventory.
Personally I would like to see something done to address the
polypiling strategy but I think if it's completely eliminated
something should be done to provide a replacement. Forcing players
to endlessly wander around the dungeon waiting for magic markers or
magic resistance cloaks to be generated may appeal to some but it
strikes me as a very dull prospect. In several hundred hours of play
I've found no more than a half dozen shields of reflection for example.
I think there are things that could be done to REPLACE the necessity
to polypile but I feel something should be added to the game if it's
simply removed arbitrarily. Below I'm reposting some suggestions that
I posted earlier on polypiling.

  My basic suggestion is that the game could would benefit form the
removal of randomly generated w.o.p.s and  some compensatory additions.

Some ideas about alternatives (perhaps 2 and 3 are mutually exclusive):

1. w.o.p. on castle level.
How about replacing the wand of wishing on the castle level with a wand
of polymorph? If I find a wand of polymorph I rarely need wishes. The
game is still playable without a w.o.p. by that stage (but more
challenging!) At the same time make the generation of nice armor and
potions more frequent (let Mordor orcs have the odd pair of gauntlets
of dexterity). Alternatively one could, for example, let captains wear
gauntlets of dexterity, master lichs have helms of brilliance, lichs or
other high level monsters have cloaks of displacement etc. This would
give some incentive for players to keep lichs, say, in the game (I
usually wipe them all out with a blessed scroll of genocide before I
even encounter one).

2. special ordering in shops.
What about allowing characters to place special orders in a shop? This
would be similar to wishing, the (crucial) difference though would be
that one would not get the item at once (and without enchantments?)
For example, an order for a pair of jumping boots might be ready after
the character has gained four additional experience levels (depending
on the item ordered). Only one extant order should be allowed (i.e.
one item per order, no additional orders until the ordered item arrives).
One might want to limit this to, say, general stores only (but it may
then be necessary to ensure that exactly one general store is generated
per game). Of course the price should probably be greatly increased
but this would give characters something to do with the gold pieces
that are so prevalent in the game (but often have so little use). It
would also eliminate a lot of the randomness that makes comparing
game scores so difficult. Similarly it would help to remove what for
me is, frankly, tedium in waiting for nice items to be randomly
generated.

3. new tools.
How about a skinning kit (sewing kit?)? I like very much that a player
can make dragon scale mail from a dragon corpse. What about extending
this? Examples - a leacrotta corpse could be made into boots of speed,
a lich (orc shaman?) corpse could be made into a cloak of magic
resistance etc. The current method for making dragon scale mail
would also be superceded by this tool.

Someone suggested before that a method to make potions analogeous to the
technique for making scrolls of choice should be added. I second this
idea, perhaps the best way would be to add another tool - a chemistry
(alchemy) set.

Another suggestion I would like to second is the addition of sandpaper to
make a shield of reflection.

Another addition over 2.3 that I really like is the addition of
intermediate goals (castle, tower etc.). What about adding small,
progressively harder, strongholds that have the suggested tools or some
of the nicer current ones (one per stronghold). These could be added
after level 15 or so.

Comments?

